    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ibn Khaldun spent some twenty years in the service of rival North African and Andalusian courts, changing sides often and going to prison for it.</li><li>In seclusion at Qalat Ibn Salama he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqaddimah">Muqaddimah</a>, which explains the rise and fall of dynasties through aṣabiyyah, the cohesion of a group.</li><li>He passed his last decades in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as a teacher and judge; his method had few followers in his time and was taken up by Ottoman and then European scholars centuries later.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, Ibn Khaldun was lowered over the city wall by ropes to negotiate with the conqueror, who questioned him in detail about the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

